"[{\"id\":\"1521811617\",\"title\":\"Can I Add a Second Ops Coordinator in a Voyage\",\"text\":\"It is possible to include a second Ops Coordinator in the Voyage Manager . To do so, you will need to enable the Configuration Flag , CFGEnableVoyageRoles . After enabling the above Configuration Flag, go to Voyage Manager > Properties and the Ops Coord 2 field becomes available for input. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"657752085\",\"title\":\"Can I change a vessel name midway through a voyage?\",\"text\":\"Home > Operations FAQs > Can I change a vessel name midway through a voyage? With the appropriate Module Rights enabled, system administrators can change a Vessel's name at any time. Impact on the Voyage The new Vessel name will only come into effect for new voyages . The old vessel name will be retained for all existing voyages . Impact on Time Chartered Vessels For time-chartered vessels that operate across voyages, changing the Vessel name midway will not update the Vessel name in the Time Charter (TC) contract. This means that invoices raised for the TC contracts will retain the old vessel name . If you would like to have the TC contract and invoices updated with the new Vessel name, you must complete the current voyage and redeliver the vessel. Then, you can create a new TC contract with the updated Vessel name for the remaining period of the TC. Impact on Estimate The estimate will show the new vessel name.\"},{\"id\":\"5001314376\",\"title\":\"Error when scheduling a TC Out- commencing port mismatch\",\"text\":\"Clicking \\\"Sched a Voyage\\\" on a TC Out contract returns a save error when the commencing port on the active voyage does not match the port set in the estimate. This is a data validation issue, not a system bug&mdash;it can be resolved by aligning the two port values. Example of error scenario: The active voyage shows \\\"AT SEA\\\" as the commencing port, but the TC Out estimate has a named port (e.g. PYEONG TAEK). IMOS requires these to match before a voyage can be scheduled and saved. [image] Voyage has a generic or placeholder commencing port . When a vessel is \\\"AT SEA\\\" at the time of scheduling, IMOS sometimes records that as the commencing port. If the TC Out estimate was built with a specific named port, the two values diverge and tigger a validation error on save. Estimate was updated after the voyage commenced If the estimate's itinerary was edited after the voyage was already in progress, the commencing port in the estimate may no longer reflect what was actualized in the voyage, causing a mismatch. To rectify this, you will need to follow the below steps: the scheduling. Open the TC Out contract that triggered the error. Navigate to the voyage itinerary for the active voyage (e.g. voyage 52). Look for the commencing port line&mdash;it may show \\\"AT SEA\\\" or a placeholder value. Update the commencing port on the voyage to match the port used in the TC Out estimate&mdash;or update the estimate to match the voyage. Either direction is acceptable as long as the two values agree exactly. Save the voyage, then return to the TC Out contract and click \\\"Sched a Voyage\\\" again. Port matching reference The table below shows how the commencing port is populated depending on the voyage status and estimate configuration. Scenario Expected commencing port Voyage scheduled, estimate has named port Must match the named port in the estimate Vessel is AT SEA at time of scheduling Update voyage to the estimate's port, or update estimate to AT SEA TCOV in progress, TCTO being scheduled Port auto-populated from last port in TCOV itinerary Estimate updated after voyage commenced Re-align estimate port to the actualized voyage port\"},{\"id\":\"4238606510\",\"title\":\"FAQ - Missing Business Rule error not showing in P&L even with missing source/bill Code\",\"text\":\"Normally, if an invoice is raised with a pair of source code and bill code that have not been added into the Business rules, a &lsquo;Missing business rule!' error will be displayed in the accounts view of the voyage P&L. Please refer to the following screenshot for an illustration: [image] However, if the invoice raised causes no impact to the P&L, then the missing business rule error will not appear. The following are the scenarios when the &lsquo;Missing business rule!' message will not appear: The invoice amount is zero OR [image] If multiple invoices are involved: All invoices must use the same pair of source code and bill code The total invoice amount must be netted off to zero. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"679609469\",\"title\":\"How can I add waypoints to a voyage itinerary?\",\"text\":\"Home > Operations FAQs > How can I add waypoints to a voyage itinerary? To insert waypoints to a voyage itinerary between standard ports, do the following: Insert a port row in between the standard ports in the itinerary. Enter an AT SEA port. Enter the Lat itude and Lon gitude coordinates. Enter one of the following Port F unctions: P : If the vessel is just passing by W : If the vessel will wait at that location Check that the Miles column has been correctly updated. If it has not, make corrections. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"713130178\",\"title\":\"How can I approve a Veslink Form on a past consecutive voyage?\",\"text\":\"This is a quick troubleshoot guideline to resolve a Veslink Form errored out because the next consecutive voyage has commenced. By using this process you will need to set the status of the current voyage from Commenced to Scheduled, this will delete all port activities entered: Change the status of all forms submitted after the target form to be submitted to \\\"Open for Resubmit\\\" Change the status of the current voyage to &lsquo;Scheduled.' The following warning message will appear: [image] All Port Activity Information from Veslink forms will be restored after Approving the previous unapproved forms. Any manually entered information will be lost. Change the Completed Voyage to Commenced. Open the target form for submission and Approve. Change the status of the Voyage to Completed. Approve all forms that were opened for resubmission.\"},{\"id\":\"4243456018\",\"title\":\"How can I create a Carbon Allowance Invoice that has a Counterparty that is not part of the TC Contract?\",\"text\":\"When the Counterparty for an Emissions invoice is not one already present within a TC contract, the field \\\"DOC Holder\\\" in the Emissions tab should be utilized. On the below example, the counterparty of the contract is company \\\"AAATESTING\\\" , but the Allowance invoice has to be issued on company \\\"TEST\\\". [image] On the \\\"Emissions\\\" tab, there is a field called \\\"DOC Holder\\\" where the company \\\"TEST\\\" can be selected from the dropdown menu. [image] Once selected and the Allowance invoice has been created, the \\\"TEST\\\" company, is now available to be defined on the invoice screen. Clicking on the \\\"counterparty\\\" field, the \\\"TEST\\\" company can be selected also there. [image] Finally, on the invoice's preview, \\\"TEST\\\" company will be reflected accordingly. [image] note 7a03833d2be6 Note: You can also set the DOC Holder directly on the contract's Emissions or EU ETS tab rather than on the invoice itself. When a DOC Holder is set at the contract level, the EU Registry ID you select there automatically defaults to new Allowance Invoices in which the DOC Holder matches the invoice's Counterparty (for TC IN and VC IN) or Company (for TCO and Cargo). For TCI cash emission invoices, the DOC Holder set on the contract defaults as the Counterparty on the invoice. Requires a schema version of 52.7. Note: You can also set the DOC Holder directly on the contract's Emissions or EU ETS tab rather than on the invoice itself. When a DOC Holder is set at the contract level, the EU Registry ID you select there automatically defaults to new Allowance Invoices in which the DOC Holder matches the invoice's Counterparty (for TC IN and VC IN) or Company (for TCO and Cargo). For TCI cash emission invoices, the DOC Holder set on the contract defaults as the Counterparty on the invoice. Requires a schema version of 52.7.\"},{\"id\":\"331612233\",\"title\":\"How can I lock estimated values in the Voyage P&L?\",\"text\":\"The Estimated column values in the Voyage P&L are mainly used as a benchmark against the voyage actual P&L. There may be business requirements to ensure the integrity of these estimated values such that they do not change through the course of the voyage. [image] You may set up the above Configuration Flag , CFGInitialSnapshotTimeSelect with one of the accepted values to perform the following: Left empty . This is the default behavior. The system will populate the Estimate column with the P&L values of the linked Voyage Estimate . ( Note: Changes made to Voyage Estimate will update the Estimated column values in the Voyage P&L.) Schedule . The system will take a snapshot of the Actual P&L values at the point of scheduling the voyage and use it to populate the Estimate column of the P&L. Commence . The system will take a snapshot of the Actual P&L values at the point of commencing the voyage and use it to populate the Estimate column of the P&L. Using this option will allow users to update the Estimate column of the P&L by using the Update Initial Snapshot function found in the Voyage P&L. [image] Setting up the Configuration Flag In the Data Center , open the Configuration Flag List . Search for CFGInitialSnapshotTimeSelect . Click the flag row to edit it. The default value of the field is empty. You may leave it empty or choose to input schedule or commence .\"},{\"id\":\"4029415500\",\"title\":\"How can I make the Worldscale Differentials reflect on the voyage?\",\"text\":\"It has been noticed that sometimes, even when the Worldscale differentials have been entered upon fixing, the same doesn't reflect on the voyage PnL. In the Estimate, when the Worldscale Differentials are entered, the Freight in the PnL will change. [image] Now, when the Estimate is fixed and a voyage is created, the Freight in the \\\"estimated\\\" column, might not reflect the same amount as in the estimate. [image] This is due to the setting on the configuration flag: CFGInitialSnapshotTimeSelect. When set to \\\"schedule\\\", a snapshot of the Voyage PNL will be taken when the voyage is scheduled, and this snapshot will be used as the basis estimate for PNL analysis. Alternatively, if set to \\\"commence\\\", this basis estimate snapshot will be taken when the voyage is commenced. So the value has to be changed to \\\"commence\\\", save and refresh. [image] Following that, open the estimate and create the voyage again from scratch. Once it's created, expand the PnL, and the \\\"estimated\\\" column reflects the same value. [image] To make the differentials reflect on the \\\"Actual\\\" column, they will either have to be added as a Misc. Rev/Exp. and included on the Freight Invoice or as an adjustment on the invoice.\"},{\"id\":\"2096594998\",\"title\":\"How can the Voyage Status for Claim Pending and Legal Hold be identified via Data Lake?\",\"text\":\"In the reporting schema, the Voyage Status field should accurately reflect those statuses. Those status values are derived from boolean fields Forecast, Legal Hold, Claim Pending, and Cancel, also in the reporting schema. In the database schema, those boolean fields are represented as bitflags 4, 6, 7, and 13, respectively, on voyage.flags: select voyage.flags & 0x0010 as forecast, voyage.flags & 0x0040 as claimPending, voyage.flags & 0x0080 as legalHold, voyage.flags & 0x2000 as cancelled from voyage When any of those flags are set (they should be mutually exclusive), that indicates the voyage status is what the flag suggests. Otherwise, the voyage status is determined by voyage.vtype, according to the mapping described in the question: blank = Scheduled F = Closed E = Completed C = Commenced (Legal Hold and Claim Pending will use status C unless set using the above)\"},{\"id\":\"700383235\",\"title\":\"How Does Loaded and Discharged LNG Cargo Impact Voyage Bunker Expenses?\",\"text\":\"Some clients load, transport, and discharge LNG as cargo while also consuming LNG on the same voyage when loading, sailing, discharging, and performing other in-port activities. Regardless of which Bunker Calculation Method is being used (FIFO, LIFO, AVE, TBM), the m3 quantity of LNG that is loaded and discharged will not impact the Voyage Bunker Expenses calculations for consumption or price of Voyage End Bunkers since it is not actively being consumed. The m3 quantities that will be allocated to the voyage as expenses will be the initial quantities from voyage commencement and burning of any additional LNG excluded from the loaded/discharged quantities. For more information on bunker expense allocation, see IMOS - Allocating Expenses to a Voyage article found in the IMOS - Operations section of the Knowledge Base. LNG ROB and Heel Pricing on Consecutive Voyages While loaded and discharged LNG cargo quantities do not affect bunker expense calculations, the Discharge Port Price and Calorific Value (CV) do affect how LNG remaining onboard is priced. When an LNG voyage completes, the Discharge Port Price and CV are applied to the LNG Remaining On Board (ROB) and heel. On a consecutive voyage, these values transfer to the Initial Price and Initial CV fields. As a result, any Boil-Off Gas BOG consumed as LNG fuel on the subsequent voyage (for example, during the ballast leg) is valued at the Discharge Port Price/CV from the previous voyage, not the original lifting price. This pricing transfer can have a significant impact on voyage P&L, particularly when the Discharge Port Price differs substantially from the price at which the LNG was originally loaded. To review or override the initial LNG price on a consecutive voyage, open Voyage Bunkers and check the LNG Detail tab .\"},{\"id\":\"714015348\",\"title\":\"How do I add drifting to my itinerary while my vessel is awaiting orders?\",\"text\":\"Tracking costs while your vessel is waiting for orders from the owners or the charterers is important. These costs need to be separated from the actual voyage sea legs to keep the estimates accurate. Therefore, we suggest the below workflow which will allow you to add an \\\" At Sea \\\" port to the voyage itinerary and define the waiting/ drifting period. System Requirement You need to enable the configuration flag CFGEnableLatLonOverride. Step-by-step workflow Go to the voyage itinerary and add AT SEA as a port to reflect where the vessel will wait for orders. You will be prompted with the following screen to include the latitude and longitude of where the vessel will be: [image] Ensure the port function is W and add the estimated waiting days to the Port Days column. The itinerary will calculate the distances to the waiting AT SEA itinerary line from the previous port. Bunker consumption while waiting will be calculated by the vessel's idle in port consumption for non-working days. Note : Depending on the Charter Party clause dictating the laytime calculation, you may choose to include waiting time in the laytime calculation, by selecting \\\" Include for waiting ports \\\". [image]\"},{\"id\":\"530875311\",\"title\":\"How do I add multiple load and discharge activities on the Cargo Handling form?\",\"text\":\"FAQs > How do I add multiple load and discharge activities on the Cargo Handling form? It is not uncommon to discharge cargo at one port while also loading more cargo later at the same port. IMOS enables you to track this data on the Cargo Handling form for each load and discharge line in the voyage itinerary. To add multiple load and discharge activities to a port: On the Voyage Manager Itinerary, click the Cargo tab. Right-click a discharge ( D ) or load ( L ) port line and then click Insert Cargo Handling Line . Save the voyage. Right-click the new itinerary line and then click Cargo Handling . Once in the Cargo Handling form, enter the cargo name, quantity, and any other pertinent information. Save the Cargo Handling form.\"},{\"id\":\"1194623465\",\"title\":\"How do I change the speed of a previously sailed leg of a voyage?\",\"text\":\"In order to change the speed of a previously sailed port or legs of a voyage you must rollback the voyage to the desired leg. To do so you must do the following: Start from the most recent port > delete port activities > delete activity report (Includes bunkers received if present) Perform the above until reaching the desired leg of the voyage Change the speed of the leg\"},{\"id\":\"1168573877\",\"title\":\"How do I delete a port from a Completed voyage?\",\"text\":\"In order to delete a port from a Completed voyage, you will need to set the voyage status back to \\\"Commenced.\\\" If it is a consecutive voyage, the current voyage status will need to be set to \\\"Scheduled.\\\" Can I change the existing port on a Completed voyage? Yes, it is possible to modify the port name directly on a voyage with a \\\"Completed\\\" status without reverting the voyage status. This functionality is intentional by system design and allows users to correct any port entry errors even after the voyage has been completed. Once the port name is updated, a confirmation prompt will appear. Select Yes to proceed. The system will automatically update the port name across all associated records, including invoices, port activities, and activity reports. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"179142907\",\"title\":\"How do I delete a Proforma DA or Final DA only?\",\"text\":\"Home > Operations FAQs > How do I delete a Proforma DA or Final DA only? Clicking the Delete button on the Port Advance/DA form will delete the entire DA. However, you may want to just delete a Proforma DA (PDA) without deleting the related Final DA (FDA) vice versa. Deleting a PDA Without Deleting Its Related FDA To delete a PDA without deleting its related FDA, first ensure that neither the PDA nor the corresponding FDA has been approved or posted. Proceed to: Clear the field PDA Amount . Change any existing value under the column Est In USD to 0. [image] Save the Port Advance/DA form. The PDA is now deleted, without affecting the FDA. Deleting an FDA Without Deleting Its Related PDA To delete an FDA without deleting its related PDA, clear the following fields: Disbursement Inv No. Disbursement Sent [image] Save the Port Advance/DA form. The FDA is now deleted, without affecting the PDA.\"},{\"id\":\"81985667\",\"title\":\"How do I delete Other Revenues/Expenses from a voyage?\",\"text\":\"Home > Operations FAQs > How do I delete Other Revenues/Expenses from a voyage? To delete an invoice line item from Voyage Other Revenues and Expenses : The invoice must not be in a posted status. You must have Read, Write, and Delete access to the Other Revenues/Expenses Module Right , under Port/Other Costs in Operations: [image]\"},{\"id\":\"875102273\",\"title\":\"How do I derive Steam Hours from Port Activities in the Report Designer?\",\"text\":\"The Steam Hours field in Port Activities is the duration of the sea leg leading to the port in hours; this is a system-derived field and not a value stored in the database. Hence, this is not a field available in the Report Designer for selection. To derive the number of Steam Hours and make it available in a report, you can create a new column and use a custom expression with values taken from the Voyage Itineraries table. See the below example: The Steam Hours at port RIO DE JANEIRO are 462.90 . [image] In the Report Designer, do the following: Add a new column . You may choose to name it Steam Hours , for example. Next, edit custom Expression field and enter the following formula: VoyageItineraries.Miles/VoyageItineraries.SpeedToPort [image] Once done, you should see the resulting value in the newly created column.\"},{\"id\":\"790036820\",\"title\":\"How do I hide reversed line items in the Final Freight Statement?\",\"text\":\"By default, the Final Freight Statement includes reversed transaction line items when incremental invoices are created for Freight or Laytime Claims. You can choose to hide these lines by enabling configuration flag CFGRemoveIncrementalReversedLinesInFinalFreight .\"},{\"id\":\"1145110619\",\"title\":\"How do I reset the voyage status to correct commence date of a consecutive voyage?\",\"text\":\"One reason why the commence date of a consecutive voyage can be incorrect is due do a previous voyage not being synced properly. To correct this you must roll back the voyage(s) to where the sync broke by setting the status to scheduled. This will automatically clear out port activities. Once setting the voyage statuses to scheduled, proceed to save the last know voyage with the correct completion date. (Ensure all voyages are selected as consecutive voyage). The voyage will now have the correct commence date.\"},{\"id\":\"1749745665\",\"title\":\"How is the field Total Tax calculated in Port Advance/DA?\",\"text\":\"There are three fields in the Total Tax Row (from left to right): Total of all tax estimates, in invoice currency. Total of all actual taxes, in invoice currency. Total of all differences between estimated and actual taxes, [image] Estimate The Total of all tax estimates, in invoice currency field is calculated by using three columns Estimate of the Port Expense (in the Advance Currency), Estimated Tax Rate, and the Estimated Tax Amount. These have been highlighted in the picture below: [image] Therefore, the total is contingent on what the total of the estimated Tax amount is. The formula for this is: Port Expense Estimate * Estimated Tax rate = Estimated Tax amount (column total) Actual The Total of all actual taxes, in invoice currency field is calculated by using three columns, Actual amount disbursed (in the disbursement currency), Actual Tax Rate, Actual Tax Amount. These have been highlighted in the picture below: [image] Therefore, the total is contingent on what the total of the actual Tax amount is. The formula for this is: Actual Amount Disbursed*Actual Tax Rate=Actual Tax Amount (column total) Difference This field is calculated by finding the difference between Estimated Tax Amount and Actual Tax Amount.\"}]"